\address{%
Department of Computer Science, Metropolitan College, Boston University, 1010 Commonwealth Avenue, \\  Boston, MA 02215, USA;\\ sidshah@bu.edu (S.S.), aish7@bu.edu (A.M.),  gourav15@bu.edu (G.S.); epinsky@bu.edu (E.P.) }

\corres{\hangafter=1 \hangindent=1.05em \hspace{-0.82em}Correspondence: epinsky@bu.edu}


\abstract{Practitioners choosing among risk measures for portfolio construction face a real question: does the choice of risk proxy---variance, Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD), interquartile range (IQR), or a wider central-quantile measure---actually change portfolio outcomes, or does it get swamped by other implementation choices? This paper investigates that question by comparing mean--variance optimization with alternatives that constrain risk using MAD, IQR, and a wider central-quantile range (P95), applied to nine U.S. sector ETFs over 27 years. All strategies share the same constraints---long-only, fully invested, with a maximum allocation cap and an explicit cash sleeve---and are evaluated across a range of annualized risk budgets and rebalancing schedules under a strict no-look-ahead timeline. The central finding is that rebalancing frequency dominates the choice of risk proxy: once these constraints are imposed, performance differences across rebalancing schedules are typically larger than differences across volatility, MAD, IQR, or P95. The robust risk proxies are feasible to implement. Still, they are not a free lunch---at longer rebalance intervals in particular, they tend to converge toward similar allocations and realized outcomes as variance-based optimization. For practitioners, this suggests that operational decisions like rebalancing cadence may deserve more attention than the choice of risk metric itself.}

\keyword{portfolio optimization; mean-variance optimization; mean absolute deviation; interquartile range; rebalancing frequency; transaction costs; sector ETFs}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{document}


\section{Introduction}

Portfolio optimization is often presented through the mean--variance framework, but implementable versions of these problems rely on risk estimates computed from finite samples of daily returns. In financial data, a small number of tail observations can disproportionately influence classical volatility estimates, and therefore materially affect the risk constraints that drive portfolio weights. In practice, this sensitivity can lead to unstable allocations across rebalances, particularly when the estimation window is short or markets experience abrupt drawdowns.

This paper studies whether simple, more robust dispersion proxies change realized outcomes in a constrained, implementable setting. The paper compares volatility-constrained optimization against alternatives that constrain mean absolute deviation (MAD), the interquartile range (IQR), and a wider central-quantile range (2.5\%--97.5\%, labeled ``P95''). These measures are attractive because they are interpretable and easy to compute on rolling windows, and because they target the dispersion of returns without allowing a handful of extreme observations to dominate the constraint by construction.

These proxies are not assumed to dominate variance-based volatility in all settings; rather, the question is whether they lead to meaningfully different allocations, turnover, and net performance once implementability constraints and estimation-window design are fixed.

Empirically, the analysis focuses on a liquid, interpretable universe of U.S. sector ETFs over a long sample (1999--2025, just under 27 years), with long-only and fully invested constraints and a maximum weight cap to avoid unrealistic concentration. The analysis evaluates annualized risk budgets (5\%, 10\%, 15\%) and rebalancing schedules (1 month, 3 months, 6 months, and 1 year) under a strict no-look-ahead timeline. Because optimized strategies can trade frequently, the paper also reports a transaction-cost robustness analysis using proportional costs that scale with turnover.

\subsection*{Motivating Evidence: Semi-Annual Outlier Inflation in Volatility}

To illustrate why robust dispersion proxies are plausible substitutes for volatility in daily-return windows, the paper summarizes the distribution of daily sector-ETF returns by semi-annual period and visualizes how volatility estimates differ when outliers are included versus removed using an IQR-based rule (\Cref{fig:motivation-outlier-inflation}). The purpose is not to present an investable signal, but to highlight a stylized fact: standard deviation computed on all observations can substantially exceed a trimmed estimate during half-years with large tail events.


\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/motivation_semiannual_outlier_inflation.png}
	\caption{Semi-annual distributions of daily simple returns for sector ETFs (pooled within each half-year). Boxes show $Q_1$--$Q_3$ with median; whiskers follow the $1.5 \times \text{IQR}$ rule; points denote outliers. The mean is marked, with overlaid $\pm 2\sigma$ bands computed on all daily returns (dashed orange) versus after removing IQR-defined outliers (dotted green).}
	\label{fig:motivation-outlier-inflation}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.} If a small number of extreme days can materially inflate dispersion estimates, dispersion-based constraints can become ``tighter'' than intended and induce larger reallocations. Robust dispersion proxies are one practical way to reduce the influence of these episodes within a daily risk constraint, but their value must ultimately be judged by net outcomes after turnover and costs.

This paper makes three contributions. First, it provides an apples-to-apples comparison of volatility-, MAD-, IQR-, and P95-constrained portfolio optimization under the same implementable constraints (long-only, fully invested, and a maximum allocation cap with an explicit cash sleeve). Second, it systematically evaluates these strategies across risk budgets and rebalancing schedules, isolating how the estimation horizon and rebalance frequency interact with both realized risk and turnover. Third, it emphasizes backtest integrity by enforcing a strict no-look-ahead timeline and by reporting outcomes both before and after proportional transaction costs, so that any apparent advantage of a risk proxy is judged on economic viability rather than on frictionless performance alone.

Three hypotheses organize the empirical work, and \Cref{sec:discussion} returns to each in turn.
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{$H_1$  (rebalancing frequency dominates).} Under a common set of implementability constraints, differences in realized outcomes across rebalancing schedules are larger than differences across the dispersion proxy used to constrain risk.
	\item \textbf{$H_2$ (proxy choice is second-order, but not negligible).} Once those constraints bind, volatility-, MAD-, IQR-, and P95-constrained portfolios produce broadly similar allocations and realized outcomes, with systematic exceptions traceable to how each proxy's risk budget is calibrated rather than to the risk concept itself.
	\item \textbf{$H_3$ (turnover is the channel through which costs act).} Because the cost model is proportional to turnover, cross-strategy differences in net performance under transaction costs are explained primarily by differences in turnover intensity.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent $H_3$ is in part a property of the cost model rather than an independent empirical finding; it is stated explicitly so that the mechanism is testable against the reported turnover figures.

\section{Related Work and Background}

Modern portfolio selection begins with the mean--variance paradigm \citep{Markowitz1952}, which formalizes the trade-off between expected return and variance, and the two-fund separation result in the presence of a risk-free asset \citep{Tobin1958}. In practice, however, implementable optimization depends on noisy, time-varying estimates of inputs---especially expected returns and the covariance matrix---\citep{bestgrauer1991,choprziemba1993,Merton1980}. A long-standing critique is that the optimizer can become an ``error maximizer,'' producing extreme allocations that are optimal for the estimated inputs but unstable out-of-sample \citep{Michaud1989}.

Two themes recur in the literature on making optimized portfolios more stable. The first is to incorporate economically motivated constraints and regularization. Even when constraints are not ``structural truths,'' they can reduce the optimizer's sensitivity and improve realized risk outcomes; long-only and position limits are canonical examples \citep{jagannathanma2003}. The second is to improve estimation. Shrinkage estimators can stabilize covariance matrices \citep{ledoitwolf2003}, and Bayesian/shrinkage approaches to expected returns can reduce the influence of noisy sample means \citep{Jorion1986}. A complementary perspective emphasizes that, in many realistic settings, simple diversified allocations can be difficult to beat out-of-sample, providing a useful benchmark for optimization-based strategies \citep{DeMiguel2009}.

This contribution sits at the intersection of these themes: implementability constraints are kept fixed, and the paper studies whether swapping the risk proxy inside the constraint---from variance-based volatility to robust dispersion measures computed on daily returns---materially changes allocations, turnover, and net performance.

\subsection{Linear and Robust Alternatives to Variance}

Variance penalizes squared deviations and is therefore sensitive to extreme observations. Because financial returns often display skewness, excess kurtosis, and tail events~\citep{Cont2001}, alternative risk measures have been widely explored. Mean absolute deviation (MAD) replaces squared deviations with absolute deviations, yielding a linear programming formulation with attractive computational properties \citep{konnoyamazaki1991}. Subsequent work studies linear programming-based portfolio optimization more broadly, including robust and downside-oriented formulations \citep{Mansini2014}. Empirically, \citet{Simaan1997} compares mean--variance and MAD portfolios under estimation risk and finds that, despite their different loss functions, the two approaches tend to produce comparable out-of-sample performance once parameter uncertainty is taken into account---so MAD's computational simplicity is not obviously purchased at the cost of realized outcomes.

Beyond MAD, a large class of robust portfolio methods modifies either the loss/risk functional (to reduce tail sensitivity) or the input estimation (to reduce parameter uncertainty). Minimax-style and related robust formulations can often be expressed in linear or convex programs, offering transparent constraint-driven control of extreme outcomes \citep{Young1998}. Quantile-based and tail-focused risk measures, such as value-at-risk and conditional value-at-risk (CVaR), provide another route to robustness by explicitly controlling tail losses; CVaR optimization is convex and has a well-known linear programming representation \citep{rockafellaruryasev2000}. Downside-risk performance measures, such as the Sortino ratio, similarly emphasize asymmetric risk \citep{sortinoprice1994}. This paper focuses on dispersion proxies (MAD, IQR, and a wider central-quantile range labeled ``P95'') that are simple to interpret and straightforward to compute on rolling windows; these measures are not intended to be the last word on tail risk, but rather a practical set of alternatives that can be embedded into a constrained, implementable optimizer.

\subsection{Turnover, Rebalancing Frequency, and Transaction Costs}

Rebalancing can improve risk control and allow the investor to respond to changing return and covariance structure, but it mechanically induces turnover. Turnover matters because trading is costly: even in liquid markets, the cost of active investing can materially erode performance \citep{French2008}. Classic theory makes this point sharply: with transaction costs, optimal policies generally involve a trade-off between tracking the desired target and avoiding excessive trading, often generating ``no-trade'' regions and gradual adjustment rather than continuous re-optimization \citep{Constantinides1986}. Building directly on this theory, \citet{DonohueYip2003} characterize the size and shape of the optimal no-trade region as a function of transaction costs, asset correlations, and risk aversion, and show empirically that calendar-based rebalancing rules---the kind of fixed-schedule policy studied in this paper---can perform close to the theoretically optimal no-trade-region policy once transaction costs are accounted for, even though such rules ignore the current distance from target weights.

From an implementation perspective, costs are not only explicit fees, but also spreads and market impact. The market microstructure literature formalizes how trading moves prices and how liquidity provision affects execution costs \citep{Kyle1985}, while optimal execution models characterize the trade-off between impact and timing when trading a target position \citep{almgrenchriss2001}. More recent dynamic portfolio choice work emphasizes that predictable returns can justify trading, but that transaction costs and risk constraints fundamentally shape optimal turnover \citep{garleanupedersen2013}. These impact- and execution-aware models are considerably more realistic than a proportional-cost assumption, but they also introduce additional parameters (e.g., impact coefficients, liquidity dynamics) that are themselves estimated with error and are difficult to calibrate consistently across nine sector ETFs, the 48 combinations of risk proxy, rebalancing schedule, and risk budget evaluated in this study, and 27 years of data. In this sector-ETF setting, the paper instead uses a simple proportional cost model applied to turnover as a deliberately transparent proxy for these frictions, prioritizing comparability across the many risk-proxy and rebalancing-schedule combinations tested over the added realism of a full market-impact model, and evaluates whether risk-proxy differences translate into economically meaningful turnover differences.

\subsection{Backtest Integrity}

Because optimization and rebalancing are sensitive to the information set, careful backtest design is essential. The backtest follows a strict no-look-ahead timeline: weights for a holding period are computed using only returns from the immediately preceding estimation window (Figure 2). This design choice ensures that the performance evaluation reflects an implementable process rather than inadvertent leakage of future information.

More broadly, the backtesting literature cautions that repeated searches over strategies, hyperparameters, and sample splits can lead to data snooping and selection bias, in which apparently strong in-sample results fail out of sample. A foundational reference is the ``reality check'' approach, which explicitly accounts for multiple testing when evaluating strategy performance \citep{White2000}. In addition, practical guidance emphasizes guarding against backtest overfitting and selection bias when comparing many strategy variants \citep{LopezdePrado2018}. This paper does not attempt a formal multiple-testing correction such as White's reality check; instead, it limits the scope for data snooping structurally, by fixing a small, pre-specified, fully crossed design---four risk proxies, three annualized risk budgets, and four rebalancing schedules---rather than searching freely over model variants, hyperparameters, or sample splits. Combined with fixed rules, explicit no-look-ahead timelines, and reported turnover/cost sensitivity, this transparency-first design is intended to reduce the scope for inadvertent overfitting, even without a formal statistical correction.

\subsection{Summary and Gap}

Taken together, prior work has largely studied these themes in isolation. Robust dispersion and quantile-based risk measures have mostly been evaluated against mean--variance either without the joint implementability constraints (long-only, fully invested, allocation caps, and an explicit cash sleeve) used here, or without systematically varying rebalancing frequency alongside the risk proxy. Conversely, the rebalancing and transaction-cost literature typically studies how often to trade a fixed target allocation, largely independent of how that target is chosen; recent empirical work on ETF portfolios, for instance, examines how rebalancing affects realized risk-adjusted returns without varying the underlying risk proxy used to set target weights \citep{banyai2024}. This paper's contribution is to jointly vary the risk proxy and the rebalancing schedule under a single, implementable set of constraints and a strict no-look-ahead backtest, which is what allows it to compare the economic magnitude of these two design choices directly rather than studying either in isolation.


\section{Data and Methodology}

\subsection{Data Used and Collected}

This paper studies a liquid and interpretable U.S. equity universe consisting of nine Select Sector SPDR ETFs:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item XLB (Materials)
    \item XLE (Energy)
    \item XLF (Financials)
    \item XLI (Industrials)
    \item XLK (Technology)
    \item XLP (Consumer Staples)
    \item XLU (Utilities)
    \item XLV (Health Care)
    \item XLY (Consumer Discretionary)
\end{enumerate}

As these nine ETFs have been in existence for the entire test period, the portfolio universe is restricted to them. Portfolio weights are chosen over this nine-asset sector universe. As a benchmark, the S\&P 500 index (\^{}GSPC) is used.

In addition, the optimizer is allowed to hold residual wealth in a cash-equivalent sleeve (CSH) with a daily return of 0. This makes cash an explicit asset in the feasible set but does not change the risky-asset universe.

Daily adjusted-close price data are collected from Yahoo Finance and merged into a single panel dataset. The sample runs from 4 January 1999 through 30 December 2025 --- just under 27 years (26.99 years) of daily data. Let $P_{t,i}$ denote the adjusted close price for asset $i$ on day $t$. The daily simple return is defined as
\begin{equation}
    r_{t,i} \triangleq \frac{P_{t,i}}{P_{t-1,i}} - 1.
    \label{eq:daily_return}
\end{equation}
Arithmetic returns are used throughout to match the backtest implementation.

\paragraph{Data and return-definition note.} Adjusted close prices reflect splits and distributions as provided by the vendor; results therefore inherit vendor conventions and any associated data-quality issues. The analysis does not model taxes or borrowing/short-rebate costs. The cash sleeve is modeled as a zero-return instrument for the entire sample; over 1999--2025, realized short-term rates varied substantially (roughly 5--6\% in 2000 and 2006--2007, near zero for extended periods in 2009--2015 and 2020--2021, and near 5\% again in 2023--2024). A flat zero return therefore understates the opportunity cost of de-risking into cash during high-rate periods, which is a relevant simplification given that binding risk budgets in this design push weight into the cash sleeve. This assumption is treated as a limitation of the present design rather than a claim that cash returns are economically negligible; a natural robustness extension is to replace the zero-return assumption with realized short-term Treasury bill returns.

\subsection{Returns and Period Construction}

Daily returns are aggregated into non-overlapping holding periods according to the chosen rebalancing schedule (1 month, 3 months, 6 months, 1 year). For each holding period, portfolio performance is computed by applying the period's weights to daily returns and compounding within the period. These non-overlapping periods are referred to as holding periods (i.e., rebalance intervals).

\subsection{Risk Measures}

All risk constraints are applied to dispersion computed on daily portfolio returns within the estimation window $\{r_{p,t}\}_{t=1}^{T}$.

\subsubsection{Volatility (Standard Deviation)}

This paper uses the sample standard deviation of daily portfolio returns as the classical variance-based dispersion proxy,
\begin{equation}
    \sigma(r_p) \triangleq \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(r_p)}.
    \label{eq:vol}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD)}

MAD replaces squared deviations with absolute deviations. For a return series $r_p$ with mean $E[r_p]$, the population MAD is
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{MAD}(r_p) \triangleq E\big[\,|r_p - E[r_p]|\,\big],
    \label{eq:mad}
\end{equation}
with the obvious sample analog used in implementation \citep{konnoyamazaki1991}.

\subsubsection{Interquartile Range (IQR)}

The IQR summarizes central dispersion via the difference between the 75th and 25th percentiles of daily returns,
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{IQR}(r_p) \triangleq Q_3(r_p) - Q_1(r_p).
    \label{eq:iqr}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Central 95\% Range (``P95'')}

As a wider central-dispersion proxy, this paper uses the central 95\% range between the 97.5th and 2.5th percentiles of daily returns (labeled ``P95'' in prose, and denoted $\mathrm{CR95}$ in equations and tables),
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{CR95}(r_p) \triangleq Q_{0.975}(r_p) - Q_{0.025}(r_p).
    \label{eq:cr95}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Implementation Details and Constraints}
\label{sec:impl-details}

\subsubsection{Notation}

Let $S$ denote the set of nine sector ETFs, and let $\mathrm{CSH}$ denote a cash-equivalent asset with daily return $r_{\mathrm{CSH},t} = 0$. The implementation uses an explicit $N=10$-asset return vector $r_t \in \mathbb{R}^N$ that stacks the nine sector-ETF returns and the cash return. A portfolio is characterized by weights $w \in \mathbb{R}^N$, producing daily portfolio returns $r_{p,t} = w^\top r_t$.

\subsubsection{Implementable Constraints}

Portfolios are constrained to be long-only and fully invested across the sector ETFs and cash:
\begin{align}
    \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i &= 1, \label{eq:fullinvest} \\
    w_i &\geq 0 \quad \forall i. \label{eq:longonly}
\end{align}
A maximum allocation constraint is imposed on sector ETFs only, to prevent extreme concentration in any single sector:
\begin{equation}
    0 \leq w_i \leq w_{\max} \quad \forall i \in S, \qquad 0 \leq w_{\mathrm{CSH}} \leq 1.
    \label{eq:capconstraint}
\end{equation}
In the implementation used for the paper build, sector-ETF weights are capped at $w_{\max} = 25\%$; this level was chosen to allow meaningful sector concentration relative to an equal-weighted 11.1\% baseline across the nine sectors, while still preventing any single sector from dominating the portfolio. The residual weight is held in cash via the full-investment constraint.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Cash modeling assumption:} The feasible set includes a cash-equivalent asset (CSH) with a daily return of 0 to allow the optimizer to de-risk by allocating to cash. This implies no earned interest on idle cash; cash is treated purely as a risk-reduction instrument. Consistent with this assumption, the risk-free rate is set to zero when reporting risk-adjusted ratios. \Cref{sec:limitations} discusses this assumption as a limitation.

\subsubsection{Optimization Objective and Portfolio Problems}

For each rebalance date, expected returns $\hat{\mu}$ are estimated as mean daily returns over the immediately prior estimation window, and weights are chosen to maximize $\hat{\mu}^\top w$ subject to a risk budget.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Solver:}  Constrained nonlinear optimization is solved using SciPy's (version 1.17.1) SLSQP routine.

\subsection{Portfolio Optimization Problems}

For each rebalance date, weights are chosen to maximize estimated mean return subject to a dispersion budget and the implementability constraints in Equations~\eqref{eq:fullinvest}--\eqref{eq:capconstraint}.
There are four portfolio optimization problems:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Volatility-constrained portfolio:} This is the standard classical Markowitz framework~\citep{Markowitz1952}
\begin{align}
    \max_w \quad & \hat{\mu}^\top w \label{eq:vol_obj} \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad & \sqrt{w^\top \hat{\Sigma} w} \leq \sigma_{\max}, \label{eq:vol_constraint} \\
    & \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i = 1, \quad w_i \geq 0 \; \forall i, \quad w_i \leq w_{\max} \; \forall i \in S. \label{eq:vol_feasible}
\end{align}

\item \textbf{MAD-constrained portfolio: } Define the sample MAD of portfolio returns over the estimation window; then solve
\begin{align}
    \max_w \quad & \hat{\mu}^\top w \label{eq:mad_obj} \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad & \mathrm{MAD}(\{w^\top r_t\}) \leq \mathrm{MAD}_{\max}, \label{eq:mad_constraint} \\
    & \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i = 1, \quad w_i \geq 0 \; \forall i, \quad w_i \leq w_{\max} \; \forall i \in S. \label{eq:mad_feasible}
\end{align}

\item \textbf{IQR-constrained portfolio:} Define the sample IQR of portfolio returns over the estimation window; then solve
\begin{align}
    \max_w \quad & \hat{\mu}^\top w \label{eq:iqr_obj} \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad & \mathrm{IQR}(\{w^\top r_t\}) \leq \mathrm{IQR}_{\max}, \label{eq:iqr_constraint} \\
    & \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i = 1, \quad w_i \geq 0 \; \forall i, \quad w_i \leq w_{\max} \; \forall i \in S. \label{eq:iqr_feasible}
\end{align}

\item \textbf{P95-constrained portfolio:} Define the sample central 95\% range of portfolio returns over the estimation window; then solve
\begin{align}
    \max_w \quad & \hat{\mu}^\top w \label{eq:p95_obj} \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad & \mathrm{CR95}(\{w^\top r_t\}) \leq \mathrm{CR95}_{\max}, \label{eq:p95_constraint} \\
    & \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i = 1, \quad w_i \geq 0 \; \forall i, \quad w_i \leq w_{\max} \; \forall i \in S. \label{eq:p95_feasible}
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Risk Budget Calibration Across Measures}

Risk budgets are parameterized at an annualized scale and mapped to measure-specific constraints, allowing different dispersion proxies to be compared under a common target risk level.

\subsubsection{Annual-to-daily risk scaling (to ensure comparability across rebalancing schedules).} 

All risk constraints are applied to risk computed on daily portfolio returns within the estimation window. Let $\sigma^{\mathrm{ann}}_{\max}$ denote the annualized risk budget (5\%, 10\%, 15\%). This is converted into a daily target via the square-root-of-time rule,
\begin{equation}
    \sigma^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max} \triangleq \frac{\sigma^{\mathrm{ann}}_{\max}}{\sqrt{252}},
    \label{eq:daily_scaling}
\end{equation}
which follows from the variance-additivity of (approximately) uncorrelated daily returns under standard modeling assumptions \citep{Campbell1997,Hull2018}. The optimizer is then constrained using risk functions computed in daily units. This design fixes the annualized risk budget across rebalancing frequencies while still using only information available at each rebalance.

\subsubsection{Measure-to-measure calibration (normal approximation).} To place MAD and quantile-range constraints on a scale comparable to volatility, normal-approximation mappings are applied to daily returns. Under a normal model, these dispersion measures are fixed multiples of $\sigma$ and can be expressed in closed form using standard normal quantiles \citep{CasellaBerger2002}:
\begin{align}
    \mathrm{MAD}^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max} &\triangleq \sigma^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max} \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}, \label{eq:mad_calib} \\
    \mathrm{IQR}^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max} &\triangleq 2\, \sigma^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max} \, \Phi^{-1}(0.75) \approx 1.349\, \sigma^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max}, \label{eq:iqr_calib} \\
    \mathrm{CR95}^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max} &\triangleq 2\, \sigma^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max} \, \Phi^{-1}(0.975) \approx 3.92\, \sigma^{\mathrm{day}}_{\max}. \label{eq:cr95_calib}
\end{align}
These mappings are used only to fix a common \emph{starting} scale across proxies at each risk budget; they do not assume that realized daily returns are normally distributed, and realized dispersion measures are reported directly rather than inferred from this mapping. Indeed, to the extent that daily sector-ETF returns exhibit fat tails (as illustrated in Section~1's motivating evidence), realized MAD, IQR, and P95 values are expected to diverge from their normal-implied levels under the volatility-equivalent budget; this divergence is itself an informative empirical outcome of the design rather than a shortcoming of the calibration. As shown in Sections~4--6, IQR in particular realizes higher volatility and higher turnover than the other three proxies at the same nominal risk budget, consistent with this calibration binding more loosely for IQR under fat-tailed daily returns.

\subsubsection{Annualization of reported dispersion measures.} 

For reporting, realized dispersion measures computed on daily returns are annualized using the square-root-of-time rule, as for volatility. For example, $\mathrm{AnnMAD} = \sqrt{252}\, \mathrm{MAD}^{\mathrm{day}}$; the same scaling is applied to IQR and the central 95\% range. This scaling is exact for standard deviation under i.i.d.\ daily returns, but is only an approximation for quantile-based measures such as IQR and CR95, whose annualization relies on the aggregated return distribution behaving approximately normally at the annual horizon rather than on an exact scaling law. Reported annualized IQR and P95 values should therefore be interpreted as approximate, comparable-scale summaries rather than exact transformations.

\subsection{Backtest Design}

\subsubsection{Rebalancing Schedules}

Rebalancing schedules of 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, and 1 year are evaluated. Each schedule defines a sequence of non-overlapping holding periods.

\subsubsection{No-Look-Ahead Timeline}

This study enforces a strict information timeline:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item At the start of holding period $k$, estimate $\hat{\mu}$ and risk inputs only from daily returns in the immediately prior period $k-1$.
    \item Solve the optimization problem to obtain weights $w_k$.
    \item Apply $w_k$ to realized daily returns within period $k$ to compute performance.
\end{enumerate}
This design ensures that weights for period $k$ do not use any return information from within period $k$.

Because the estimation window is defined as the immediately prior holding period, the estimation window length equals the holding period length for each rebalancing schedule.

\subsubsection{Rebalance-day mechanics.} 

Weights $w_k$ become effective at the start of period $k$; proportional transaction costs (when modeled) are applied immediately before earning period-$k$ returns. \Cref{fig:no-lookahead} summarizes this timeline.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/no_lookahead_timeline.png}
	\caption{Backtest timeline. Inputs for period $k$ weights are estimated using only period $k-1$ returns; weights are then held during period $k$.}
	\label{fig:no-lookahead}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Estimation Window Length and Rebalancing Frequency}

Tying the estimation window to the holding period, as described above, means that shorter rebalancing schedules are estimated from correspondingly shorter return histories --- for example, the 1-month schedule estimates all risk measures from roughly 21 daily observations. This is a deliberate design choice: it keeps the estimation window strictly aligned with the information an investor would actually have accumulated since the last rebalance, avoiding an arbitrary separate choice of lookback length. However, it also means that quantile-based measures, and P95 in particular, are estimated with substantial noise at short windows, since a 2.5th/97.5th-percentile estimate from 21 observations is close to the sample minimum and maximum. This raises a specific concern for interpreting this paper's central finding: if rebalancing frequency appears to dominate risk-proxy choice partly because short-window quantile estimates become unstable or degenerate, that would be an estimation-noise artifact rather than a genuine finding that the proxies are economically interchangeable at high rebalance frequency. This paper treats the coupling of window length to holding period as a transparent, implementable baseline design, and flags the decoupling of estimation-window length from rebalancing frequency (e.g., using a fixed lookback regardless of holding-period length) as an important robustness check for disentangling these two explanations.

\subsubsection{Transaction Costs and Turnover}

This paper models proportional transaction costs that scale with turnover. Let $w_k$ denote weights at rebalance $k$; define charged turnover (total traded notional in risky assets as a fraction of portfolio value) as
\begin{equation}
    \tau_k \triangleq \sum_{i \in S} |w_{k,i} - w_{k-1,i}|,
    \label{eq:turnover}
\end{equation}
where $S$ is the set of sector ETFs (excluding the cash sleeve CSH). This corresponds to charging a one-way fee per trade on risky assets while treating the cash leg as free: reallocations between cash and a risky asset incur a one-way fee, whereas reallocations between risky assets incur both a sell and a buy fee.

For a transaction cost rate $c$ in basis points (bps), a rebalance-time cost proportional to traded notional is applied. In the baseline robustness analysis, $c \in \{0, 1, 2\}$ bps is used (equivalently 0.00\%, 0.01\%, 0.02\% per unit traded). The rebalance haircut is
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{Cost}_k \triangleq c\, \tau_k\, V_{k^-},
    \label{eq:cost}
\end{equation}
where $V_{k^-}$ is portfolio value immediately before rebalancing. When the cash sleeve is absent (or fixed at zero), buys and sells in risky assets match in aggregate, and the corresponding one-way risky-asset turnover is $\tfrac{1}{2}\tau_k$.

\subsection{Performance Metrics}

Performance is evaluated using both level and risk-adjusted measures.

\subsubsection{Terminal Wealth and Portfolio Value}

Let $V_t$ denote portfolio value; the terminal $V_T$ is reported along with time-series plots of $V_t$.

\subsubsection{Risk and Risk-Adjusted Return}

Realized volatility, realized MAD, and realized IQR are reported. Risk-adjusted performance is summarized using an explicit return-to-volatility ratio:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{CAGR/AnnVol} \triangleq \frac{\mathrm{CAGR}}{\mathrm{AnnVol}},
    \label{eq:cagr_annvol}
\end{equation}
where CAGR is used as the numerator to align with long-horizon compounding in the backtest. This explicit label (CAGR/AnnVol) is used rather than the conventional Sharpe ratio, which is typically defined using mean excess returns. Table and figure columns headed ``CAGR/AnnVol'' throughout this paper refer to this ratio, not the conventional Sharpe ratio.

\subsubsection{Drawdown}

Two drawdown measures are reported. The \emph{full-sample maximum drawdown} is computed from the running peak-to-trough decline of the entire daily cumulative wealth path $V_t$, without reference to rebalance boundaries:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{MDD}^{\mathrm{full}} \triangleq \min_{t \leq T} \left( \frac{V_t}{\max_{s \leq t} V_s} - 1 \right).
    \label{eq:mdd_full}
\end{equation}
Because it is boundary-free, it captures declines that straddle two holding periods, and its measurement window does not depend on the rebalancing schedule. It is therefore comparable across strategies, risk budgets, and rebalancing schedules, and it is the statistic on which every cross-schedule drawdown comparison in this paper is based. The \emph{within-period maximum drawdown (within-period MDD)} applies the same peak-to-trough definition separately to the daily return path of each holding period, and the worst such value across periods is reported. This measure resets at every rebalance boundary, which gives it two properties that must be kept in mind. First, it cannot see a decline that begins in one holding period and continues into the next. Second, it mechanically admits deeper values at longer holding periods, because a longer window contains more of any given peak-to-trough move. Its measurement window is the holding period itself and therefore varies by construction along the rebalancing dimension, so within-period drawdowns are \emph{not} comparable across rebalancing schedules. They are retained as a per-period diagnostic and for continuity with the period-level metric grids, not as a basis for cross-schedule inference.

\subsubsection{Turnover}

Turnover levels by strategy and rebalance schedule are reported as a key driver of transaction cost sensitivity.

\section{Empirical Results: 0 bps Transaction Costs}
\label{sec:results-0bps}

This section presents the core results in a frictionless setting (0 bps proportional transaction costs), emphasizing how outcomes vary by risk proxy and rebalancing schedule at a fixed, pre-specified annualized risk budget. The paper presents the main figures and summary table in-text, followed by the complete per-metric grids across the full risk-budget and rebalancing grid.

Because the backtest evaluates a grid of specifications (risk budgets, frequencies, and risk proxies), Table~\ref{tab:summary-zero-cost} below reports, for each strategy and rebalancing frequency, results at the risk budget that maximizes terminal portfolio value (a ``best-of-three'' view across the 5\%/10\%/15\% grid); the fixed-10\%-budget view used for direct cross-proxy comparison and the anchoring example below is reported separately in Table~\ref{tab:tc-summary} (Section~\ref{sec:tc-robustness}). Results at the other risk budgets are reported in full in the per-metric grids included in \Cref{sec:results-0bps,sec:weights-turnover-0bps,sec:tc-robustness} and are referenced in-text where they materially change the interpretation.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{What to look for in a frictionless setting:} 
Before introducing transaction costs, it is useful to separate two questions: (i) whether rebalancing frequency changes performance mechanically through estimation-window length and how often the optimizer is ``allowed'' to react, and (ii) whether swapping the dispersion proxy inside the risk constraint materially changes outcomes once implementability constraints (long-only, max-weight cap, and an explicit cash sleeve) are imposed. The results below provide direct evidence on both dimensions and motivate why turnover will become the dominant channel once proportional costs are introduced.


\subsection{Summary of frictionless results}

Table~\ref{tab:summary-zero-cost} reports, for each strategy and rebalancing frequency, the realized outcome at the risk budget that maximizes terminal portfolio value (sorted by frequency, then strategy), together with realized risk, drawdown, and turnover. The corresponding full grids across all three risk budgets are reported in Tables~\ref{tab:app-terminal-pv-0}--\ref{tab:app-netpv-2} below.

