Three patterns stand out.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Rebalancing frequency is first-order.} Across the optimized strategies, 6-month rebalancing often produces the strongest terminal wealth outcomes in this sample; monthly optimization performs materially worse in terminal wealth despite satisfying the same annualized risk-budget targets.
	\item \textbf{Risk proxy choice is usually second-order, but not negligible.} At longer rebalance intervals (6 months and 1 year), the four risk proxies often produce comparable realized volatility and turnover, with performance differences that are modest relative to the effect of rebalancing frequency. As documented in Sections~\ref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}--\ref{sec:tc-robustness}, IQR is a consistent partial exception: it realizes higher volatility and higher turnover than the other three proxies at the same nominal risk budget in nearly every cell of this grid. Its terminal-wealth advantage is narrower --- IQR leads under monthly and 6-month rebalancing, while P95 leads at the quarterly and annual horizons.
	\item \textbf{Turnover is mechanically high for optimization.} Even at 0 bps, monthly optimization generates turnover near 1 per rebalance (sum of absolute \emph{non-cash} weight changes), which foreshadows substantial cost sensitivity once proportional frictions are introduced (\Cref{sec:tc-robustness}).
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Discussion.}
The first two patterns indicate that, in this constrained sector-ETF universe, the choice of rebalancing schedule can dominate the choice of dispersion proxy in realized economic outcomes. In particular, intermediate rebalance intervals appear to strike a balance between (i) having enough data in each estimation window to reduce noise and (ii) updating weights often enough to react to changing return and covariance structure.
The third pattern is the key mechanism for the cost analysis: frequent re-optimization generates frequent trading, so even small proportional costs can compound into meaningful drag.

\Cref{fig:pv-zero-cost} plots the cumulative wealth paths behind these summary figures. To anchor magnitudes at a \emph{fixed} 10\% risk budget (rather than the best-of-three view in Table~\ref{tab:summary-zero-cost} above), the IQR strategy reaches a terminal portfolio value of 657.18 under 6-month rebalancing versus 371.10 under monthly rebalancing (with the S\&P benchmark at 469.37; these fixed-10\%-budget figures are reported in full in Table~\ref{tab:tc-summary}, Section~\ref{sec:tc-robustness}, at the 0 bps column). The corresponding turnover levels (reported in \Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost}) are substantially higher under monthly rebalancing.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/pv_curves_zero_cost.png}
	\caption{Gross portfolio value paths (0 bps transaction costs) at a 10\% annualized risk budget. Panels correspond to rebalancing schedules (1mo, 3mo, 6mo, 1y); each panel compares strategies on the same dollar scale.}
	\label{fig:pv-zero-cost}
\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
Even before costs, the rebalance interval changes the effective signal-to-noise ratio of inputs (because the estimation window equals the holding period) and can dominate which strategy ``wins'' in terminal wealth.


\subsection{Terminal wealth}

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap-pv-zero-cost} summarizes terminal wealth across all risk budgets and rebalancing schedules for each strategy.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/heatmap_terminal_pv_zero_cost.png}
	\caption{Terminal gross portfolio value heatmap (0 bps transaction costs). Each panel shows one strategy; axes represent annualized risk budgets (vertical) and rebalancing schedules (horizontal). Cell values indicate the terminal portfolio value in dollars.}
	\label{fig:heatmap-pv-zero-cost}
\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{Relative performance and patterns.}
Two patterns stand out in \Cref{fig:heatmap-pv-zero-cost} and \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}. First, the dominant axis is \emph{rebalancing frequency}: for the optimized strategies, the 6-month schedule is often associated with the largest terminal wealth across risk budgets in this sample, though there are exceptions (e.g., some annual configurations under the P95 proxy). Monthly and quarterly optimization tend to underperform, even though they satisfy the same annualized risk-budget targets.
Second, differences across dispersion proxies are present but typically smaller than the rebalance-interval effect. Within a fixed rebalance interval, the IQR-constrained strategy delivers the highest terminal wealth among the optimized variants at the monthly and 6-month horizons, while P95 leads at the quarterly and annual horizons; volatility and MAD are frequently close to one another. The P95 proxy is sometimes competitive at the annual rebalance horizon, suggesting that proxy choice can matter most when the optimizer is asked to make infrequent, larger allocation decisions.

\noindent\textbf{Benchmark comparison.}
The S\&P buy-and-hold benchmark provides a useful reality check: it is difficult for short-interval optimization (1mo/3mo) to overcome the combination of estimation noise and constrained implementation in this sector-ETF universe. Outperformance relative to the benchmark appears primarily in the 6-month configurations (and, more narrowly, in some annual configurations), reinforcing the practical idea that optimization benefits from longer windows and less frequent re-trading.

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
If you are choosing between ``risk proxies'' inside a constrained optimizer, the heatmap suggests you should first choose a rebalance interval that is implementable and cost-aware; proxy choice is often a second-order tuning decision in this universe, though IQR specifically warrants monitoring realized (not just nominal) risk, as discussed further in Sections~\ref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}--\ref{sec:tc-robustness}.


