	\label{fig:heatmap-mdd-zero-cost}
\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{The cross-schedule drawdown pattern is a measurement artifact.}
On the within-period measure, drawdowns appear to grow sharply with the rebalancing interval. That pattern is a property of the measurement window rather than of the strategies: a longer holding period simply contains more of any peak-to-trough move, and the buy-and-hold benchmark---whose exposure never changes---exhibits the same apparent pattern ($-28.52\%$, $-35.19\%$, $-42.36\%$, $-48.01\%$ in \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}) purely as a function of how the sample is cut. On the boundary-free measure the pattern largely disappears. At the 15\% budget, full-sample drawdowns run from $-34.9\%$ to $-36.8\%$ at the monthly schedule, $-36.7\%$ to $-39.6\%$ at quarterly, $-33.6\%$ to $-35.6\%$ at semiannual, and $-41.7\%$ to $-45.4\%$ at annual. These ranges overlap substantially and do not order monotonically in the rebalancing interval. The gap between the two measures is largest at the monthly schedule, where the within-period figures ($-13.2\%$ to $-14.2\%$) describe only the worst single month while the deepest decline actually realized is at least $-34.9\%$ for every proxy; a monthly reset cannot see a drawdown that runs for years. Rebalancing more often did not deliver materially shallower drawdowns in this sample. This does not weaken the paper's central claim, which rests on terminal wealth, turnover, and net-of-cost outcomes; it does establish that drawdown is not one of the dimensions along which the rebalancing decision separates.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Relative to the benchmark.}
Every constrained configuration reduced the worst realized decline substantially relative to buy-and-hold. The S\&P~500 fell $-56.78\%$ from 9 October 2007 to 9 March 2009, against $-13.9\%$ to $-45.4\%$ for the optimized portfolios across all risk budgets and rebalancing schedules. That reduction is attributable to the risk budget and the cash sleeve---both common to all four proxies---rather than to the choice of dispersion measure.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Across dispersion proxies.}
Within a given risk budget and rebalancing schedule the spread across the four proxies is modest and the ordering is not stable. IQR posts the deepest drawdown in nine of the twelve (risk, frequency) cells and ties for deepest in a tenth, which is consistent with the calibration discussion in Section~3.6: its looser effective budget under fat-tailed returns shows up as deeper drawdowns as well as higher realized volatility. Overall, swapping volatility for MAD, IQR, or P95 dispersion does not systematically \emph{reduce} tail losses within this constrained implementation (long-only, max-weight cap, and an explicit cash sleeve).

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Why several drawdowns are identical across proxies.}
A number of configurations report drawdowns that agree to the cent: all four proxies record $-36.68$ at the quarterly schedule and $-33.55$ at the semiannual schedule in \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}. This is not a reporting artifact but a direct consequence of the constrained design. The dispersion constraints in Equations~\eqref{eq:vol_constraint}, \eqref{eq:mad_constraint}, \eqref{eq:iqr_constraint}, and \eqref{eq:p95_constraint} are inequalities. When a constraint is slack, the corresponding program reduces to maximizing $\hat{\mu}^\top w$ subject only to $\sum_i w_i = 1$ and the box $0 \leq w_i \leq w_{\max}$---a linear program whose solution places weight at the cap on the highest-mean assets and does not depend on which dispersion measure was written into the constraint. In sufficiently calm estimation windows the constraint is slack for every proxy and all four portfolios coincide exactly. At the 15\% budget this occurs in 58\% of monthly periods, 51\% of quarterly periods, 46\% of semiannual periods, and 35\% of annual periods; the corresponding figures at the 10\% budget are 24\%, 15\%, 8\%, and 4\%. The quarterly and semiannual drawdown ties are such cases: the weights held through the first quarter of 2020 were set from the calm fourth quarter of 2019, no proxy's constraint bound, and all four portfolios held an identical capped allocation into the COVID-19 selloff. Because \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost} selects the 15\% budget in fifteen of its sixteen optimized rows, a substantial share of that table sits in this degenerate regime. This mechanism is itself evidence for the paper's central claim: where the risk budget does not bind, the choice of risk proxy cannot matter at all, and the outcome is determined entirely by the feasible set and the rebalancing schedule.

\medskip
