\subsection{Realized risk and risk-adjusted performance}
To keep the main text streamlined, it focuses on realized annualized volatility and a return-to-volatility ratio (CAGR/AnnVol, with $r_f=0$), both reported alongside performance in \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}. Alternative proxy-specific return-to-risk ratios and the related diagnostic grids are reported in full below.

\noindent\textbf{Relative performance and patterns.}
Two consistency checks are useful when interpreting the performance grids. First, realized dispersion generally increases with the stated risk budget across all strategies, indicating that the annual-to-daily scaling and proxy-to-proxy calibration produce broadly sensible monotonic behavior in realized risk. Second, across the four optimized strategies, realized risk levels are often quite similar within a (risk, frequency) cell, as expected given that all optimizers face the same implementation constraints and target dispersion budgets on the same underlying daily-return data --- with the notable exception of IQR, which realizes consistently higher volatility than the other three proxies at the same nominal target (\Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}), a pattern consistent with the normal-approximation calibration binding more loosely for IQR under fat-tailed returns (Section~3.6).

In terms of risk-adjusted performance, differences across proxies are present but not uniform across rebalance intervals. Higher realized volatility does not by itself explain higher terminal wealth: the ordering of configurations by terminal wealth is driven primarily by rebalance interval, not by realized dispersion. Within a fixed interval, however, IQR is a systematic exception --- it carries both the highest realized volatility and, at the monthly and 6-month horizons, the highest terminal wealth, which is why its apparent advantage is better read as looser realized risk-taking than as a superior risk concept. This reinforces the paper's central theme: in a constrained, implementable setting, the \emph{interaction} between estimation window length and rebalance frequency can dominate the incremental choice of dispersion proxy, though IQR's elevated realized risk is a specific, explainable exception worth tracking rather than averaging away.


\noindent The complete per-metric grids underlying these statements are reported in \Cref{tab:app-vol-0,tab:app-mad-0,tab:app-iqr-0,tab:app-sharpe-0,tab:app-sharpe-mad-0,tab:app-sharpe-iqr-0,tab:app-rar-vol-0,tab:app-rar-mad-0,tab:app-rar-iqr-0}: realized dispersion under each of the three proxies, and both the conventional and proxy-specific return-to-risk ratios, for every risk budget and rebalancing schedule.

