\noindent The results above summarize \emph{performance} at 0 bps. The discussion next turns to the \emph{portfolio mechanics} behind these outcomes---weight paths and turnover---because these will be the primary channel through which proportional transaction costs affect net performance (\Cref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps,sec:tc-robustness}).

\section{Portfolio Weights and Turnover (0 bps)}
\label{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}
This section focuses on weight distributions and turnover across rebalancing frequencies and strategies.
These allocation dynamics provide the foundation for the transaction cost analysis in \Cref{sec:tc-robustness}.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Evidence and interpretation.}
If proportional transaction costs scale with turnover, then weight dynamics are not merely descriptive: they determine the economic viability of a strategy. This section documents two empirically visible mechanisms.
First, turnover falls mechanically as the rebalancing interval increases, because weights are adjusted less frequently. Second, differences across risk proxies tend to appear through cash usage and concentration patterns rather than through completely different sector exposures --- though, as detailed below, IQR is a partial exception on the turnover dimension specifically.

Beyond these headlines, two additional points are important for interpretation. First, ``low turnover'' does not necessarily mean ``small trades'': lower-frequency portfolios often exhibit \emph{lumpier} reallocations (larger step changes at each rebalance) even if average turnover per rebalance is lower. Second, because estimation windows are defined as the immediately prior holding period, higher-frequency portfolios operate with shorter samples and can therefore exhibit more parameter noise, which mechanically increases trading as inputs fluctuate.

\subsection{Turnover across frequencies and strategies}
\Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost} reports turnover (mean rebalance-to-rebalance sum of absolute weight changes) across risk budgets and rebalancing schedules at 0 bps. The 10\% risk budget is highlighted as a representative ``medium'' case in the discussion below.

Turnover is computed as the rebalance-to-rebalance sum of absolute \emph{non-cash} weight changes (i.e., $\tau_k=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}} |w_{k,i}-w_{k-1,i}|$), consistent with the transaction-cost model in \Cref{sec:tc-robustness}. This treats the cash sleeve as a funding source/sink with no direct trading fee.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Discussion.}
The monotone decline in turnover from 1-month to 1-year rebalancing provides a direct mechanism for why higher-frequency strategies can lag once proportional costs are introduced: the same cost rate is applied to a larger cumulative traded notional when turnover is persistently high (\Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost,fig:cost-drag,tab:tc-summary}).

\noindent Two patterns in \Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost} are especially relevant for the cost analysis.
First, \emph{frequency dominates}: even when turnover per rebalance is only modestly higher at 1-month than at 1-year, there are many more trading dates. A simple back-of-the-envelope annualized ``turnover intensity'' is $m\,\bar{\tau}$, where $m\in\{12,4,2,1\}$ is the number of rebalances per year and $\bar{\tau}$ is mean turnover per rebalance. At 10\% risk, the volatility strategy has $\bar{\tau}\approx 0.858$ at monthly rebalancing but $\bar{\tau}\approx 0.517$ at annual rebalancing; this implies roughly $12\times 0.858\approx 10.3$ turnover units per year versus $1\times 0.517\approx 0.5$ turnover units per year --- a roughly 20-fold relative difference. In absolute terms this translates into only about 0.10\% versus 0.005\% of annual cost drag at a 1 bps proportional cost rate (rising to roughly 0.21\% versus 0.01\% at the 2 bps rate used elsewhere in this paper); both figures are individually small, and it is the relative multiplier, not the absolute drag at the lowest cost tier, that constitutes the mechanism illustrated here.

Second, \emph{risk budget matters more at short rebalance intervals}. At monthly rebalancing, allowing higher risk budgets leads to noticeably higher turnover (e.g., volatility: 0.586 at 5\% vs 0.858 at 10\% vs 0.957 at 15\%, a spread of 0.371). At annual rebalancing, the same cross-risk spread is smaller (e.g., volatility: 0.405 at 5\% vs 0.517 at 10\% vs 0.650 at 15\%, a spread of 0.245), suggesting that infrequent trading naturally limits how aggressively the optimizer can reposition.

\paragraph{IQR turnover is systematically higher, not merely comparable.} Across nearly every cell of \Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost}, IQR has the highest turnover among the four dispersion proxies, at times by a substantial relative margin --- for example, at 5\%/6mo, IQR turnover is 0.593 versus Volatility's 0.388 (roughly 53\% higher); at 5\%/1mo, IQR is 0.765 versus Volatility's 0.586 (roughly 30\% higher). This pattern is consistent with the calibration observation in Section~3.6: IQR also realizes higher volatility than the other three proxies at the same nominal risk budget, consistent with a systematically looser realized constraint for IQR under the normal-approximation calibration. A proxy operating under a looser realized budget would be expected to both take more risk and trade more to do so, which is exactly the joint pattern observed here. Section~\ref{sec:tc-robustness} tests directly whether this elevated turnover translates into a correspondingly larger transaction-cost penalty for IQR.

