\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
Turnover is not just a diagnostic --- it is an input to net performance. A strategy that looks competitive at 0 bps can become unattractive once turnover is multiplied by a realistic trading-cost assumption, and this appears especially relevant for proxies, such as IQR, that combine higher frictionless returns with higher turnover.




\subsection{Weight distributions through time (10\% risk budget)}
To isolate how the choice of dispersion proxy affects portfolio composition at a fixed risk budget, \Cref{fig:weights-area-strategy-10} plots sector and cash weights over time for each strategy at a 10\% risk budget (0 bps, monthly rebalancing).

\noindent\textbf{Discussion.}
Across strategies, the implementability constraints and the presence of a cash sleeve visibly limit extreme concentration, so the most salient differences tend to arise in (i) how aggressively the optimizer uses cash to satisfy the risk budget and (ii) how concentrated the sector mix becomes during particular regimes.
In economic terms, the cash sleeve acts as the optimizer's ``risk dial'': when the estimated dispersion of risky assets rises, the optimizer can satisfy the risk budget by shifting weight into cash rather than by forcing extreme sector shorts (which are disallowed). This generates a natural interpretation of proxy differences.
If a proxy treats a given episode as ``riskier'' (because of tail observations or a wider dispersion estimate), the optimizer tends to lean more heavily on cash and reduces active sector tilts; if a proxy is less sensitive, it tends to remain more fully invested and expresses risk through a larger risky allocation.

The figures also emphasize a practical point about implementability: the portfolios rotate across sectors over time, but the rotations are smooth and bounded by the cap constraint. As a result, much of the cross-strategy variation is in \emph{how much} active risk is taken (cash usage and concentration) rather than \emph{which} sectors are held.

