\subsection{Alternative cost structures and extensions}
The proportional cost model used in this analysis is a first-order approximation. Several extensions would provide a more realistic representation of trading frictions, though at the cost of additional complexity.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Nonlinear market impact:} 
A well-established result in market microstructure is that price impact grows nonlinearly with trade size \citep{almgrenchriss2001,Kyle1985}. In a simplified permanent-impact model, executing a trade of size $Q$ in an asset with daily volume $V$ incurs an impact proportional to $(Q/V)^\alpha$ for some $\alpha \in (0.5, 1)$, often calibrated near 0.5--0.6 for equities. For the liquid sector ETFs in this study, impact is likely negligible at modest rebalance sizes, but large reallocations (e.g., moving 10--20\% of portfolio value into a single ETF in a single day) could incur measurable impact.

If impact were modeled, the main qualitative result---that turnover drives cost drag---would persist, but the penalty for high-turnover strategies would be amplified because large discrete trades (which occur more frequently at short rebalance intervals) would be disproportionately expensive. This would further favor lower-frequency rebalancing, and would likely widen, rather than narrow, IQR's cost disadvantage documented above, since IQR is both the highest-turnover proxy and the one most associated with larger reallocations.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Time-varying spreads and volatility-dependent costs:} 
Spreads widen during stress periods and contract during calm markets. A more refined model could make $c$ time-varying, linked to measures of market liquidity or realized volatility. This would penalize rebalancing during crises (when dispersion estimates are noisiest and spreads are widest) and could interact with the risk-proxy choice: if robust proxies reduce the frequency of ``panic reallocations,'' they might deliver a secondary benefit through lower realized execution costs.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Optimal execution and temporary impact:} 
The current model assumes trades execute instantaneously at a fixed cost. In practice, large orders are split over time to minimize the temporary impact, creating a trade-off between urgency and cost \citep{almgrenchriss2001}. Incorporating optimal execution would require specifying a within-rebalance trading horizon (e.g., trade over 1--2 days after the signal), which would smooth the realized cost but also introduce timing risk.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Turnover penalties and no-trade regions:} 
A direct way to mitigate turnover is to add an explicit turnover penalty to the objective or to impose no-trade bands that prevent rebalancing unless weight drift exceeds a threshold. These approaches are standard in practice and can materially reduce trading intensity \citep{garleanupedersen2013}. The current analysis does not implement such mechanisms, but the turnover diagnostics in \Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost} provide a baseline against which turnover-penalized variants could be compared. Given IQR's elevated turnover documented above, it is plausibly the strategy with the most to gain from such a mechanism, though confirming this is left to future work.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Implications for robust proxies:} 
If nonlinear impact or time-varying costs were introduced, the relative ranking of risk proxies could shift further if one proxy systematically avoids large trades during high-cost regimes. Based on the turnover and cost-drag patterns already observed in this implementation (\Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost,tab:tc-summary}), such a shift would plausibly work \emph{against} IQR rather than in its favor, since IQR is already the highest-turnover, highest-relative-drag proxy under the simple linear cost model used here.

