\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
In this implementation, the cost curve is largely a turnover curve: reducing rebalance frequency (and/or explicitly penalizing turnover) is a more direct lever for improving net outcomes than swapping among closely related dispersion measures. Among the dispersion measures themselves, IQR's higher frictionless returns come paired with a consistently higher cost sensitivity; in this sample the former outweighs the latter, but the margin of that trade-off narrows as trading frictions are assumed to be more severe.

