\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
This study highlights a practical hierarchy for portfolio construction in a constrained, implementable optimizer. The empirical results are best interpreted not as a ``horse race'' among dispersion formulas, but as evidence about which 
\emph{design levers} matter most in a realistic workflow that repeatedly (i) estimates inputs from finite windows, (ii) solves a constrained optimization problem, and (iii) trades to targets under costs.

Three high-level takeaways summarize the discussion.
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Constraints and estimation windows are first-order.} Long-only, fully-invested portfolios with position limits can materially stabilize optimization \citep{jagannathanma2003}. In this setting, the estimation-window design (here: ``previous holding period'') and rebalance frequency jointly determine how noisy inputs are and how often the optimizer is allowed to react, and these effects can dominate incremental changes in the dispersion proxy.
	\item \textbf{Risk-proxy mappings are useful but imperfect.} Calibrating MAD/IQR/P95 budgets to an annualized ``risk target'' relies on distributional approximations (here: normal multiples). When returns are non-normal or time-varying, different proxies can bind in different regimes, so cross-proxy comparisons should be read as comparisons of \emph{implementations} rather than as statements of dominance of one risk concept over another. As documented in Sections~\ref{sec:results-0bps}--\ref{sec:tc-robustness}, this is not merely a theoretical caveat: IQR realizes systematically higher volatility, higher turnover, and higher relative transaction-cost drag than the other three proxies at the same nominal risk budget, consistent with its normal-approximation-implied constraint binding more loosely than intended under fat-tailed daily returns.
	\item \textbf{Turnover is the primary channel for costs.} The transaction-cost robustness analysis reinforces the classic point that active policies must be evaluated net of trading frictions \citep{French2008}. In these sector-ETF optimizations, frequent re-optimization mechanically increases traded notional, and even 1--2 bps can compound into economically meaningful drag.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Why proxy differences compress --- but do not fully disappear --- under constraints}
In unconstrained settings, volatility and robust dispersion measures can imply meaningfully different optimal solutions because the constraint set can ``tilt'' the optimizer away from tail-sensitive estimates. In this paper's constrained setting, several mechanisms compress those differences, though they do not eliminate a consistent, if economically modest, IQR-specific pattern.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{The cash sleeve acts as a shared risk valve.} Because cash is available and earns a deterministic 0 return, every proxy can satisfy its risk budget by adjusting the \emph{risky share} (sector ETFs) versus cash. If one proxy estimates an episode as riskier, the optimizer can respond by increasing cash rather than by producing extreme offsetting sector positions (which are disallowed). This induces a common pattern: proxy differences show up primarily in cash usage and concentration intensity.
	\item \textbf{The max-weight cap prevents ``error-maximizing'' concentration.} A cap such as $w_{\max}=25\%$ limits the optimizer's ability to express small input differences via extreme positions. As a result, distinct dispersion constraints can lead to similar feasible corner solutions, especially at longer horizons where input estimates are less noisy.
	\item \textbf{The objective is mean return with noisy estimates.} Because $\hat\mu$ is estimated from short, rolling daily windows (equal to the holding period), the optimization is often driven by estimation noise in $\hat\mu$ subject to a binding risk constraint. Under this structure, changing the risk proxy can shift which constraint binds and how much cash is used, but it does not necessarily create a persistent ``alpha'' mechanism.
	\item \textbf{The normal-approximation calibration is an imperfect equalizer.} The mechanisms above compress differences \emph{conditional on} each proxy's constraint being calibrated to an equivalent effective risk level. Because that calibration relies on a normal approximation (Section~3.6), it does not guarantee equal realized risk-taking across proxies when daily returns are fat-tailed. This is the most likely explanation for why IQR, specifically, consistently realizes more risk, more turnover, and a larger relative cost penalty than the other three proxies, even though the compression mechanisms above hold for all four.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Design choices vs. empirical findings}
Two experimental design choices drive much of the cross-frequency behavior.
First, the estimation window equals the immediately prior holding period, so higher-frequency strategies use shorter samples to estimate inputs. This makes both $\hat\mu$ and dispersion estimates more variable, increasing the probability of frequent reallocations.
Second, identical implementability constraints apply to all proxies (long-only, full investment, a sector-only cap, and an explicit cash sleeve), which intentionally isolates the effect of changing the dispersion proxy but also tends to push solutions toward similar feasible allocations.

An implication is that the paper's ``rebalance frequency dominates'' finding should be read as a joint statement about (i) rebalance frequency and (ii) the specific information set induced by the estimation window (see also Section~3.7.3). Alternative window choices (e.g., a fixed-length trailing window independent of holding period) could change the frequency comparison by decoupling ``how often you trade'' from ``how much data you estimate with.''

\subsection{Regime dependence and economic interpretation}
While the paper does not run a formal subperiod attribution, the largest shifts in allocations and turnover occur in well-known stress episodes (e.g., the early-2000s drawdown, the 2008--09 crisis, and 2020). In such regimes, robust proxies can dampen the influence of a handful of extreme daily observations inside the dispersion constraint, which tends to translate into higher cash allocations and (in some cases) less aggressive sector rotation.

However, in this constrained sector-ETF setting, the dominant driver of net outcomes remains the rebalance schedule and the turnover it induces. Robust dispersion proxies therefore function primarily as \emph{implementation tools}---a way to reduce tail sensitivity of the risk constraint---rather than as a standalone source of persistent outperformance.

\subsection{Practical implementation guidance}
For a practitioner deciding how to operationalize a constrained optimizer of this type, the results suggest a pragmatic sequence.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Pick a rebalance policy that is cost-aware first.} Start with a frequency that is operationally feasible and consistent with expected trading frictions; use turnover and a cost model as primary diagnostics.
	\item \textbf{Choose an estimation window intentionally.} If the goal is stability, consider decoupling the window length from the holding period (e.g., using a longer trailing window but trading less frequently).
	\item \textbf{Then tune the risk proxy, with an eye on realized (not just nominal) risk.} MAD/IQR/P95 can be valuable if risk budgets are intended to be robust to rare shocks, but proxy choice is unlikely to rescue an otherwise high-churn process. If IQR is used, monitor realized volatility and turnover against the other proxies' realized levels, since the calibration mapping used here does not guarantee identical realized risk-taking across proxies.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations and robustness checks}
\label{sec:limitations}
There are several limitations worth emphasizing, both to frame the conclusions appropriately and to clarify which extensions are most likely to change the results.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Universe and scope.} Results are for a narrow and interpretable sector-ETF universe under long-only, fully-invested constraints with a sector-only cap and a cash sleeve; findings may not generalize to broader universes, leverage, shorting, or factor portfolios. The evidence is also U.S.-only, equity-only, and drawn from a single 1999--2025 sample: no international, fixed-income, or multi-asset universe is examined, and no out-of-sample or alternative-period validation is performed.
	\item \textbf{Expected return estimation.} Optimized portfolios are sensitive to expected return estimates \citep{Merton1980}. Using a raw rolling mean of daily returns is intentionally transparent but noisy; shrinkage/regularized mean estimators, Bayesian approaches, or signal-combination frameworks could reduce churn and potentially change the relative performance of rebalance frequencies.
	\item \textbf{Covariance and dependence structure.} The volatility strategy uses a sample covariance estimator in each window; results may differ under shrinkage covariance estimators or regime-dependent models \citep{ledoitwolf2003}. Likewise, the square-root-of-time scaling assumes weak dependence; volatility clustering and serial correlation can cause realized annualization to deviate from $\sqrt{252}$ scaling.
	\item \textbf{Risk-budget calibration across measures.} The proxy-to-proxy mapping uses normal multipliers to set comparable constraint levels. This is a pragmatic calibration, not a structural truth; when distributions exhibit skewness/kurtosis, the mapping can cause one proxy to bind more tightly --- or, as observed for IQR in this sample, more loosely --- than another in specific regimes, directly affecting cash usage, turnover, and the cost sensitivity documented in Section~\ref{sec:tc-robustness}.
	\item \textbf{Transaction cost model.} Proportional costs are a stylized proxy for spreads and frictions. A more refined model could incorporate time-varying spreads, volatility-dependent execution costs, and market impact that grows nonlinearly with traded notional \citep{Kyle1985,almgrenchriss2001}. The current model is best viewed as a sensitivity analysis that isolates the role of turnover.
	\item \textbf{Drawdown measurement.} Full-sample maximum drawdowns are reconstructed from the saved rebalance weights and daily asset returns rather than computed natively inside the backtest; the reconstruction reproduces every stored within-period value exactly across all 5{,}928 strategy-periods, but promoting it to a first-class pipeline metric would be a worthwhile build-level refinement. The drawdown column of \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost} and the drawdown heatmap remain within-period quantities and are not comparable across rebalancing schedules; all cross-schedule drawdown conclusions in this paper are drawn from \Cref{tab:mdd-fullsample} only.
	\item \textbf{Turnover is measured target-to-target.} Turnover is computed as the absolute change between the target weights set at successive rebalance dates, not between the newly chosen targets and the weights actually held after intra-period drift. Realized turnover is therefore understated, and understated most at the longest holding periods, where drift between rebalances is largest. Since the analysis concludes that infrequent rebalancing is preferable partly on cost grounds, this bias runs in favor of that conclusion and should be corrected before the cost estimates are treated as precise.
	\item \textbf{Benchmark asymmetry.} The benchmark is the S\&P 500 index itself, which bears neither an expense ratio nor trading costs, while the strategies hold ETFs whose adjusted closing prices are already net of fund expenses. Over a 27-year sample this asymmetry is of the same order as the transaction-cost drag being studied, and it works against the optimized strategies; an investable proxy would place both sides on equal footing.
	\item \textbf{Cash yield and taxes.} The cash sleeve earns 0, and the analysis ignores taxes. In practice, cash yields can materially affect the attractiveness of de-risking, and after-tax turnover can amplify the cost disadvantage of high-frequency trading.
	\item \textbf{Robustness to alternative timelines.} The strict no-look-ahead design is a strength, but other implementable variants exist (e.g., overlapping estimation windows, delayed execution, or using end-of-day signals with next-day implementation). These can affect both turnover and realized performance.
	\item \textbf{Additional robustness checks (natural extensions).} Three particularly relevant checks are (i) decoupling the estimation-window length from the holding period (e.g., a fixed 6- or 12-month trailing window regardless of rebalance frequency), to test whether the ``rebalance frequency dominates'' finding survives independently of the estimation-noise confound flagged in Section~3.7.3; (ii) penalizing turnover explicitly in the objective (or adding trading bands/no-trade regions), following the norm-constrained portfolio framework of \citet{DeMiguelNorms2009}, to test whether the cost sensitivity --- particularly IQR's --- can be mitigated mechanically; and (iii) varying the cap $w_{\max}$ and the presence/return of cash to quantify how much of the proxy similarity is driven by feasible-set design.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Economic vs. statistical significance}
The paper primarily focuses on \emph{economic} significance: whether a strategy's edge survives implementability constraints and trading frictions, and whether differences are large enough to matter in terminal wealth, drawdowns, realized risk, and turnover.
This paper reports point estimates only: no confidence intervals, standard errors, or formal tests of differences across proxies or across rebalancing frequencies are computed, and no claim of statistical significance is made anywhere in what follows. Every comparison below should be read as descriptive of this sample. The emphasis on economic magnitude is deliberate, because small changes in compounded wealth over multi-decade samples can be economically meaningful, but it is not a substitute for inference.

Statistical significance is more difficult in portfolio backtests because returns are time-dependent and the effective sample size can be small, especially at low rebalancing frequencies where there are few non-overlapping holding periods. In addition, repeated evaluation over a grid of configurations (risk budgets, frequencies, proxies) introduces a multiple-comparisons/data-snooping concern \citep{White2000,LopezdePrado2018}.

A publication-grade extension would therefore report uncertainty using block-resampling methods over holding periods (or other dependence-aware resampling) and would test differences in risk-adjusted performance using measures aligned to the stated objective. For example, one could report tests on differences in Sharpe-like ratios defined on mean excess returns \citep{jobsonkorkie1981} (while acknowledging that the paper's main ratio uses CAGR/AnnVol for interpretability over long horizons). More generally, the correct inference target depends on the investor's utility and horizon: what matters is not whether one proxy ``wins'' a t-test, but whether the improvement is stable, implementable, and robust to plausible perturbations in estimation and costs.

\subsection{Hypothesis evaluation}
The evidence across the 0 bps results (\Cref{sec:results-0bps}), the weight/turnover diagnostics (\Cref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}), and the transaction-cost robustness analysis (\Cref{sec:tc-robustness}) supports a unified empirical takeaway, restated below to reflect the IQR-specific pattern documented across those sections.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Hypothesis 1 (frequency dominates).}
Rebalancing frequency and the information set implied by the estimation-window design are first-order determinants of outcomes. Intermediate rebalance intervals (notably 6 months in this sample) often deliver stronger frictionless terminal wealth than shorter intervals, plausibly because the longer estimation window reduces estimation noise in $\hat\mu$ and the risk constraint, while less frequent trading reduces churn; this holds within the shared implementability constraints (full investment, a sector cap, and an explicit cash sleeve) applied to all strategies. This pattern is also consistent with the theoretical no-trade-region literature: \citet{DonohueYip2003} show that fixed-schedule rebalancing can approximate the performance of the theoretically optimal, cost-aware no-trade policy, so an intermediate calendar frequency outperforming a higher-frequency one is in line with that theory's prediction that trading more often than the optimal no-trade boundary requires is a net drag once transaction costs are considered, rather than evidence against fixed-schedule rebalancing as a class.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Hypothesis 2 (proxy choice is second-order, but not negligible, under implementability constraints).}
Once long-only, full investment, a sector cap, and an explicit cash sleeve are imposed, changing the dispersion proxy typically affects allocations through cash usage and mild concentration shifts rather than through radically different sector exposures. Across many (risk, frequency) cells, realized outcomes are broadly similar across volatility, MAD, IQR, and P95, especially at longer horizons, and the rebalance-frequency effect is typically --- though not universally --- larger than the proxy effect. At the 6-month horizon and a 10\% risk budget, for instance, the spread across proxies (657.18 for IQR versus 440.76 for volatility) exceeds the spread across frequencies for the volatility proxy (440.76 at 6 months versus 317.62 at 1 month). However, this should not be read as full equivalence: IQR realizes higher volatility, higher turnover, and a larger relative transaction-cost penalty than the other three proxies at the same nominal risk budget in nearly every cell (\Cref{sec:results-0bps,sec:weights-turnover-0bps,sec:tc-robustness}), a pattern consistent with the normal-approximation calibration binding more loosely for IQR rather than with a genuine advantage of the IQR risk concept. The present design does not vary the calibration, so it cannot separate these two explanations. Proxy choice is therefore second-order in magnitude relative to rebalancing frequency, but is not economically inert.

\noindent
\noindent\textbf{Hypothesis 3 (turnover explains cost sensitivity).}
Because the cost model is proportional to turnover, net performance under costs is largely explained by turnover intensity. Higher-frequency strategies exhibit persistently larger cumulative traded notional, which explains why even low single-digit basis-point costs can materially change net rankings; the same mechanism explains why IQR, as the highest-turnover proxy, also exhibits the largest relative cost drag at every rebalancing frequency tested.

For exposition, the 10\% risk budget is treated as a representative ``medium'' case when illustrating allocation paths and cost drag, while retaining the full (risk, frequency, strategy) grid in the summary and turnover tables.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper evaluates volatility-, MAD-, IQR-, and central-quantile-range (P95)-constrained portfolio optimization in a constrained, implementable sector-ETF setting over a long historical sample and under a strict no-look-ahead timeline. The analysis emphasizes the interaction between (i) how risk is measured in daily windows, (ii) how often the portfolio is re-optimized, and (iii) how turnover translates into net performance under costs.

The core message for practitioners is a hierarchy of decisions.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Choose a rebalance interval and estimation window jointly.} In this universe and timeline (estimation window equals the holding period), differences across 1mo/3mo/6mo/1y schedules are typically larger than differences across dispersion proxies (Sections~\ref{sec:results-0bps}--\ref{sec:tc-robustness}). More broadly, the window length determines input noise; the rebalance frequency determines how often that noise is ``traded.''
	\item \textbf{Treat turnover as a primary risk alongside drawdown and volatility.} When proportional costs are introduced---even at 1--2 bps---net outcomes are largely explained by turnover intensity. A configuration that looks attractive at 0 bps can lose its edge once repeated trading haircuts compound (Section~\ref{sec:tc-robustness}).
	\item \textbf{Use robust dispersion proxies as implementation choices, not silver bullets.} MAD/IQR/P95 constraints are feasible and interpretable, but in this constrained ETF setting, they do not reliably deliver a systematic turnover advantage relative to volatility constraints. If anything, IQR --- which posts the strongest frictionless terminal wealth under monthly and 6-month rebalancing, though not at the quarterly or annual horizons --- also carries a modest but consistent turnover and cost-drag disadvantage relative to the other three proxies (Sections~\ref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}--\ref{sec:tc-robustness}), a pattern most plausibly explained by the normal-approximation calibration binding more loosely for IQR under fat-tailed daily returns (Section~3.6) rather than by any inherent advantage of the IQR risk concept itself. Any incremental benefit from proxy choice is most likely to come from (i) better expected-return estimation, (ii) explicit turnover-aware design (penalties, bands, or no-trade regions), or (iii) a different objective that directly trades off expected return against both risk and trading costs.
\end{itemize}

From an implementation lens, the practical implication is straightforward: if the objective is to improve net terminal wealth and deliver a robust investor experience, prioritizing a cost-aware rebalance policy and reducing unnecessary trading is likely to be more impactful than swapping among closely related dispersion measures inside an otherwise unchanged optimizer. Robust dispersion proxies remain useful---particularly as guardrails against outlier-driven tightening of risk constraints---but the results suggest that turnover-aware design is the more direct lever for economic viability in this setting.

These conclusions are drawn from a single, narrow universe (nine U.S. sector ETFs), a specific set of implementability constraints, and a stylized proportional cost model; Section~7.5 details the corresponding limitations and the robustness checks --- decoupling the estimation window from the holding period, penalizing turnover explicitly, and varying the allocation cap and cash treatment --- that would most directly test whether this hierarchy of decisions holds outside the present design.

